# Moonlight OS compositor configuration.
#
# This is deliberately not sway's shipped default config: that one binds most
# of the keyboard for a tiling desktop, and every one of those bindings would
# be a key the streamed host PC never receives.  An appliance that forwards
# almost the whole keyboard wants the opposite -- exactly one binding.
#
# sway is started by moonlight-session on tty1 after autologin.  There is no
# display manager.

### --- appearance ---------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here is ever meant to look like a desktop.  No decorations, no gaps,
# no status bar: a single fullscreen client on a black background.
default_border none
default_floating_border none
titlebar_border_thickness 0
hide_edge_borders both
font pango:DejaVu Sans 11

output * bg #000000 solid_color

# Replaces unclutter: the pointer disappears when it stops moving rather than
# sitting over the stream.
seat * hide_cursor 3000

# A stream is not a desktop -- the pointer crossing a window edge should never
# move focus away from what is being watched.
focus_follows_mouse no

### --- X11 ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Off on purpose.  Selene is a native Wayland client (Qt's wayland QPA plus
# SDL's wayland video driver), and nothing else in the image needs X, so
# leaving Xwayland enabled would ship an X server to run nothing.
#
# If a future client turns out to need X, this is the single line to flip.
xwayland disable

### --- the settings panel -------------------------------------------------
# The whole reason the X11 session had a triggerhappy daemon reading
# /dev/input: while streaming, the client grabbed the X keyboard, so the
# window manager never saw this combination and the panel could not be opened.
#
# A compositor binding is evaluated before the key reaches any client, so that
# entire mechanism -- triggerhappy, its evdev rules, the setuid-ish hand-off
# script, the iconify-to-drop-the-grab dance -- collapses into this one line.
#
# --to-code binds the physical key position rather than the letter, so this
# still lands on the key labelled M under an AZERTY layout, where it would
# otherwise be the semicolon.  That replaces the duplicated KEY_SEMICOLON
# rules the evdev version needed.
bindsym --to-code Ctrl+Alt+m exec /usr/local/bin/moonlight-panel

# ...and the client is not allowed to take that binding away.
#
# Wayland has a direct analogue of the X keyboard grab: a client may ask the
# compositor to stop processing its own shortcuts, via
# zwp_keyboard_shortcuts_inhibit.  SDL implements SDL_SetWindowKeyboardGrab
# with it, and the client turns that on while streaming (capturesyskeys), so
# by default sway stops honouring the binding above at exactly the moment it
# is needed -- the same failure the X11 grab caused, in a new protocol.
#
# Refusing the inhibitor is what keeps the panel reachable mid-stream.  The
# cost is that the two combinations bound here no longer reach the host PC;
# every other key still does.  Under X11 they reached both, because reading
# /dev/input is passive.
seat * shortcuts_inhibitor disable

# Panic exit, matching the old session's Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Q.
bindsym --to-code Ctrl+Alt+Shift+q exit

### --- window placement ---------------------------------------------------
# The panel gets a workspace to itself, and the compositor switches to it as
# the window appears.
#
# It cannot simply open on top.  A second toplevel over a fullscreen client
# does not reliably get drawn: sway reports the panel fullscreen and visible
# and the client not visible, and the screen still shows the client.
#
# That is a compositing bug rather than a display one -- grim reads back what
# wlroots composited, and in the failing case grim returns the client, so the
# wrong surface really was composited.  Nothing at the DRM layer is involved:
# this output runs transform=90 and wlroots cannot scan out a rotated output,
# so direct scanout was never in play, and neither
# WLR_SCENE_DISABLE_DIRECT_SCANOUT, WLR_SCENE_DEBUG_DAMAGE=rerender,
# WLR_DRM_NO_ATOMIC nor WLR_DRM_NO_MODIFIERS makes any difference.
#
# A layer-shell surface on the overlay layer is the mechanism that is actually
# defined to draw above fullscreen surfaces, and it works here (checked with
# swaynag -y overlay over a live stream).  foot cannot use it -- no layer-shell
# support -- so until the panel stops being a terminal, it goes where the
# client isn't.  See moonlight-panel for the full write-up.
#
# Done here rather than in moonlight-panel because a script that switches
# workspace and then spawns the terminal races: sway places a window on
# whatever is focused when it maps, which is not reliably the workspace the
# script just asked for.  As one rule on the window itself, there is no gap.
for_window [app_id="moonlight-panel"] move container to workspace mlos-panel, workspace mlos-panel, fullscreen enable

### --- notifications -----------------------------------------------------
# What draws the low-battery warning over a running stream.  It has to be the
# notification daemon rather than anything of ours, because layer-shell is the
# only way to paint above a fullscreen client without taking focus from it.
exec mako

### --- drop-ins -----------------------------------------------------------
# Settings the menus write out so they survive a reboot -- the touchpad's
# scroll direction, for one.  Included last so a drop-in wins over anything
# above it.
include /etc/sway/config.d/*

### --- the session --------------------------------------------------------
# Applies the saved layout, keymap and input settings, then runs the client.
# When it exits, sway exits, and moonlight-session takes over on the console.
exec /usr/local/bin/moonlight-wlsession
